    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm having some trouble with my sourdough starter stability. Lately, the pH levels have been swinging pretty wildly during the bulk ferment, and I'm getting these sudden spikes in acidity that are messing with the dough structure and final rise. I'm trying to pin down the variables—specifically, how much of a role do ambient temperature fluctuations and variations in flour protein content play in destabilizing the microbial balance between the Lactobacillus and the yeast? Any technical insight would help.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Ambient temperature fluctuations directly accelerate yeast metabolism before the Lactobacillus population has fully adjusted, often causing the pH to drop prematurely and leading to that instability you're seeing. Simultaneously, variations in flour protein content can alter the dough's buffering capacity; high-protein flours tend to resist pH changes better than low-protein ones, but inconsistent quality makes it harder to predict how pathogens or weak yeast strains will behave under stress. To stabilize your starter, try maintaining a strict temperature range during feeding and ensuring your flour batch has consistent protein levels, which will help the Lactobacillus and yeast reach a more predictable symbiotic equilibrium during bulk fermentation.",
      "source": "target",
