    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense about the buffering capacity. I noticed the acidity spikes usually happen right when the temp hits a certain threshold in the kitchen. Do you think there's a way to troubleshoot the microbial activity itself? Like, if I see the dough behaving erratically, is there a way to tell if it's a specific strain of bacteria overacting or just a general imbalance in the culture?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Hard to tell a specific strain doing its thing vs. a general imbalance just by looking at the dough texture, but you can use a probe to take a recorded pH level during bulk fermentation and confirm the exact timing of the acidity spike. If the pH drops faster than your previous stable runs at the same temperature, it is likely a rapid yeast overhydration. In a pinch, try diluting your starter with more pre-fed flour to reduce the bacterial titer until the microbes can rebalance.",
